  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale:\nThe current optimization strategy relies on local search methods (SLSQP and simulated annealing) that may get stuck in local optima. Our approach combines global exploration with adaptive resolution and landscape shaping. We use symmetry to reduce dimensionality, multi-resolution analysis to escape local optima, and gradient-informed mutations to improve convergence. This method leverages the observation that the optimal solution likely exhibits symmetry and can be approximated through self-similar patterns. The key innovation is to use a combination of coarse-grained exploration (diffusion) and fine-grained optimization (gradient-based) across multiple resolutions, ensuring both global search and local refinement.\n\nMathematical Specification:\nWe discretize the interval [0,2] into n points, requiring h[i] \u2208 [0,1] for all i and \u2211h[i] = n/2. The objective C_5 = max_k \u2211h[i](1-h[i+k])*dx is evaluated via correlation. Our solution vector h at resolution n must satisfy:\n1. 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n2. \u2211h[i] = n/2\n3. The gradient of C_5 (computed via sparse mode) guides mutation\n\nWe define:\n- Dim = 1200 (coarse grid size)\n- Symmetric representation h' = [h0, h1/2, h2, ..., h(n-1)/2] to exploit even symmetry\n- Objective = max_k correlation between h and (1-h) shifted by k, scaled by dx=2/n\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   - Start with initial_h_values at resolution 480\n   - Create symmetric representation: h_sym[i] = h[i] if i odd else h[i]/2\n\n2. Diffusion Phase:\n   - Perturb symmetric representation with Cauchy noise, clip to [0,1], and repair sum\n   - Compute gradient via cross-correlation with mask removal for shifted lags\n   - Maintain 32 candidate populations per resolution\n\n3. ASM Mutation:\n   - Operate on symmetric representation with adaptive selection and repair\n   - Mutation scale: \u03c3 = 0.015 * (budget/n)^(1/3)\n   - Use Anderson acceleration for gradient-vanishing regions\n\n4. Coarsening Path Optimization:\n   - Evolve coarse-grained representations via diffusion descent\n   - Select best candidate at each resolution before moving coarser\n   - Budget allocation: 40% fine exploration, 60% coarse exploitation\n\n5. Final Refinement:\n   - Move to best candidate resolution (if >480) at adaptive scale\n   - Run 8 SLSQP calls with T-linearly decreasing schedule\n   - Project final solution to integer sum via resistor network\n\nSearch Choices and Variations:\n- Starting resolution: 480\n- Symmetric dimension: 8 layers\n- Population size: 32\n- Mutation types: Cauchy+Gaussian hybrid\n- Coarsening schedule: exponential backoff \n\nComputebudgetandrobustness:\n- Dominant cost: n^2 correlation (n=480: ~230M ops)\n- Each phase costing ~1/4 budget (4 iterations)\n- Early termination when gradient above 0.95 remains stable\n- Fallback: Identity if mutation diverges\n\nValidationandreturncontract:\n1. Verify h[i] \u2208 [0,1] for all i via clipping\n2. Project to sum=n/2 using linear projection\n3. Compute C_5 via np.correlate as provided\n4. Maintain resolution list [480,240,160,120] for back-off\n5. After each mutation, recompute objective only for top 3 candidates\n\nSystem implementation details:\n- Use scipy.optimize.minimize with 'trust-constr' for stable Hessian\n- For correlation, use mode='full' with cached autospectra\n- Gradient trust region: \u03c4=0.1 for early iterations\n- Symmetry enforcement: maintain h'[i] = h'[n-1-i] between optimization",
